      "writtenOn": "2025-04-15T23:41:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Bleh... it\u0027s pretty hard to understand all the conditions and how they are branching and pruned right now. I had to read it quite a few times to convince myself this is correct.\n\nI think the most jarring condition that tripped me up was that `!queueLowestWaitSerials.empty()` implies `timeout \u003d\u003d Nanoseconds(0) || queueLowestWaitSerials.size() \u003d\u003d 1` by the second if block clause because of the early out. It was especially confusing because immediately following it is the `else if (timeout \u003d\u003d Nanoseconds(0))`.\n\nI think it would be a bit easier to follow if we split the logic by whether we are polling or waiting, even if we end up repeating the call a little bit, so something like:\n\n```\n// Handle polling.\nif (timeout \u003d\u003d Nanoseconds(0)) {\n    success |\u003d WaitQueueSerials(queueLowestWaitSerials, futures, timeout);\n    success |\u003d PollFutures(futures);\n    return success ? wgpu::WaitStatus::Success : wgpu::WaitStatus::TimedOut;\n}\n\n// Handle waiting with a timeout, where only a single type of event is allowed.\nif (!queueLowestWaitSerials.empty()) {\n    DAWN_ASSERT(queueLowestWaitSerials.size() \u003d\u003d 1);\n    success |\u003d WaitQueueSerials(queueLowestWaitSerials, futures, timeout);\n} else if (foundSystemEvent) {\n    // Can upgrade wait list events to system events.\n    success |\u003d\n        WaitAnySystemEvent(\n            SystemEventAndReadyStateIterator{futures.begin()},\n            SystemEventAndReadyStateIterator{futures.end()}, timeout);\n} else if (foundWaitListEvent) {\n    success |\u003d \n        WaitListEvent::WaitAny(\n            WaitListEventAndReadyStateIterator{futures.begin()},\n            WaitListEventAndReadyStateIterator{futures.end()}, timeout);\n} else {\n    DAWN_ASSERT(futures.size() \u003d\u003d 0);\n}\n```",
